# Wren Ashdown, the bard of the distant city (Act 2 opening). Spawned at
# the lead's settlement by TSC_Act2_BardLead; meeting her completes the
# quest via the TSC_MetBard signal. Act 2 proper picks up from here -
# this scene establishes her, the song, and where the song comes from.

dialogue TSC_Dialogue_Bard

# In the party: she rides with you, lute and all.
entry joined if in_party()
entry met if flag(TSC_MetBard)

== start ==
You find her by following the crowd: a common room packed shoulder to shoulder, and on the table by the hearth a woman with a battered lute, mid-verse. The song is about a king, and a crown, and the nine pieces it broke into.

She sees your gear, or maybe what your gear has been through, and finishes the verse looking straight at you. The room empties slowly. She doesn't.

"Wren Ashdown," she says. "You're no farmer come for the chorus. Ask."

* "Where did you learn that song?" -> song_source
* "Those verses are Undercrown canon. One manuscript, in a locked vault. Explain that." -> song_source
    if flag(TSC_OswinLeadGiven)
* [Say nothing. Set the shard on the table.] -> shard_reveal

== shard_reveal ==
She looks at it a long time. She does not touch it. When she finally speaks, the performer is gone and something warier is sitting in her place.

"So it's a true song," she says quietly. "I'd half convinced myself I made it up. That's the trouble, you see. I don't remember learning it."

* "Start from the beginning." -> song_source

== song_source ==
"Everyone asks that, and I lie to everyone, because the truth doesn't rhyme." She sets the lute down, which reads like a door being locked. "Two winters ago I sang for coppers by the old cistern stairs, down where the city forgets to sweep. There was a beggar there who spoke in couplets. Old meter. I thought it was madness with good scansion."

"He went down the stairs one night - down, courier, where the cellars flood and honest people don't - and he never came back up. And a month later I caught myself singing verses I'd never written, in a meter I'd never studied, about a crown I'd never heard of. The song came *up* those stairs with me. I just can't tell you what carried it."

* "Then the stairs are where I'm going." -> met_first
    do flag(TSC_MetBard); signal(TSC_Act2_BardLead, TSC_MetBard); affinity(+5)
* "You're coming with me to look at those stairs." -> met_first
    do flag(TSC_MetBard); signal(TSC_Act2_BardLead, TSC_MetBard)

== met_first ==
"The cistern stairs. The sunken cellars under the old city." She picks the lute back up, hugging it more than holding it. "I'll draw you the way. I'll even sing at your funeral, courier, if it comes to that - I'll have the material."

She pauses at the edge of a thought.

"Whatever taught a beggar Undercrown canon is still down there. Songs have to come from somewhere."

She turns the lute over and draws on the back of it, in charcoal, without apology: a square, a stairhead, three lines going down.

"That's the village. That's the stair. I've never been past the third step, and I've stood at the top of it more nights than I'll admit to."

* "Then we go down. Mark it properly." -> lead_taken
    do give_quest(TSC_Act2_Cellars); grant_xp(25)
* "Come with us, Wren. The song isn't finished, and we need your help." -> recruit
* [Take your leave.] -> end

== lead_taken ==
She blows the charcoal dust off the lute and hands you the map, which is to say she hands you her instrument, looks at what she has done, and takes it back to copy onto a scrap of paper like a sane person.

"There'll be water. There was always water at the bottom of the steps, even in a dry year." She hesitates over the last thing, then says it anyway. "Courier - if there's someone still down there singing it, don't lead with the sword. That's all. Just don't lead with the sword."

* "Understood." -> end
* "Come with us and tell them yourself." -> recruit

== met ==
Wren is where the crowd is, as bards are. She lifts two fingers off the lute's neck in greeting.

"Still above ground. Good. The stairs are where I said, and the song hasn't changed its mind."

* "Sing the crown verses again. All of them." -> met_song
* "Come with us, Wren. The song isn't finished, and we need your help." -> recruit
* "Just passing through." -> end

== recruit ==
She stops playing. For a while she looks at the lute instead of you, and when she answers it's quieter than her singing voice.

"I've spent two winters being scared of this song. Singing it in taverns with the door in reach." Her thumb finds a worn spot on the lute's neck. "But it's *mine*, isn't it. Whatever carried it up those stairs gave it to me, and I've been paying that debt in tavern coppers."

She stands, and the fear is still there, but it has moved to the back of the queue.

"Someone has to be there to sing the ending. All right, courier. I ride with you - me, the lute, and the only true song left in the world."

* "Welcome to the company, Wren." -> end
    do join_party(); affinity(+5); grant_xp(50)
* "Think on it. I'll ask again." -> end

== joined ==
Wren rides with the party the way she performed: watching everything, keeping the rhythm. The lute travels wrapped in oilcloth like a relic, which by now it might be.

"Courier. Need a verse, or just company?"

* "Sing the crown verses. All of them." -> met_song
* "How are you holding up, away from your cozy tavern?" -> joined_talk
    if not flag(TSC_WrenRoadTalk)
* "Just company." -> end

== joined_talk ==
"Cold. Saddle-sore. Closer to the song than I've ever been." She thinks about it properly, because you asked properly. "In the taverns I performed it. Out here it feels less like singing and more like *following*. Like the verses are ahead of us on the road, leaving tracks."

She shrugs the fear off her shoulders, practiced by now.

"Ask me again when we're standing where the next verse happened. I'll tell you if I regret this."

* "Fair enough, singer." -> end
    do flag(TSC_WrenRoadTalk); grant_xp(10); affinity(+5)

== met_song ==
She sings it complete this time, unhurried: the king, the shattering, the nine roads the pieces took. Verses about a crypt in the hills - you know that one now. Verses about water, and cellars, and a thing that waits in the wet dark. And verses at the end in a meter so old it drags its feet, which she sings with her eyes closed and cannot translate.

"That last part isn't mine," she says afterward. "It isn't anyone's. That's the part that scares me."

* "It's a map, Wren. All of it." -> end
    do grant_xp(25)
